## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

Your code contains three patterns requiring migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Prevents rethrow to keep thread alive | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |

### Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime — it simply doesn't work. The runtime no longer supports forcibly injecting exceptions into threads. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation** where the code voluntarily checks for and responds to cancel requests.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same as the old ThreadAbortException block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed. We control whether to continue or exit
                // via the loop condition. Loop will exit because token is cancelled.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Give thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Explained

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Signals cancellation cooperatively |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | Not needed — you control flow via the loop condition |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2))` | Actually waits for the thread instead of hoping |

---

### Critical: Fix `GetNextItem` to Accept the Token

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), cancellation **will not take effect** until it unblocks. You must pass the token through:

```csharp
// Before: blocks forever, can't be cancelled
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // BlockingCollection
}

// After: wakes up when cancelled
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
}
```

If you're using `Thread.Sleep` inside `GetNextItem`:
```csharp
// Replace Thread.Sleep with cancellation-aware wait
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(millisecondsTimeout);
```

---

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `Stop()` hangs because the thread is stuck in `GetNextItem` forever — cancellation never fires |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter** | Accidentally swallows cancellation from unrelated subsystems |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` runs for 30 seconds without checking, you wait 30 seconds |
| **Using `Thread.Interrupt()` as a substitute** | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9) and unreliable — use the token |

---

### If `ProcessItem` Is Long-Running

Add checkpoints inside it:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
{
    DoStepOne(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    DoStepTwo(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    DoStepThree(item);
}
```

---

### Validation Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort` — builds without `SYSLIB0006`
- ✅ No `ThreadAbortException` catch blocks
- ✅ No `Thread.ResetAbort`
- ✅ Token passed through the full call chain
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` disposed in `Stop()`
- ✅ Blocking calls accept the token so they can be interrupted
